<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ratnasimha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1303">1303</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ratnasimha of Mewar, Rawal Ratan Singh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ratnasimha was the last ruler of the main Guhila line of Mewar, reigning at Chittor from about 1302. In 1303 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> besieged Chittor for about eight months and took the fort, and Ratnasimha was killed or captured. Later legend turned him into Ratansen, husband of the queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmini">Padmini</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-muhammad-jayasi">Malik Muhammad Jayasi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmavat">Padmavat</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ratnasimha.jpg" alt="Ratnasimha" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssjoshi111 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chittorgarh_fort.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
